14875d647cc9add42a49426b5d6322bf3d91","file":"preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026/build/main.tex","line":63,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"6e5c744a0cd311f07462b31b5fba01ff304d34b7e3643338d2e70a6f45bf7cde","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026/build/main.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:dd3092e2ea37f646e5eb610884eb14875d647cc9add42a49426b5d6322bf3d91","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Bounded-Step-Walks-on-Gaussian-Primes-September-26-2026/build/main.tex#L63"},"statement_kind":"theorem","text":"\\label{thm:main}\\label{cor:uniform}\nFor every finite real $D$ there is a finite $B_D$ such that every\nconnected component of $G_D$ has at most $B_D$ vertices.\nThe bound is independent of the starting prime and includes primes on\nthe coordinate axes. Consequently every sequence of distinct Gaussian\nprimes whose successive distances are at most $D$ has at most $B_D$ terms."}
